  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Maine total $2.8B for FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.6B and dominates the state’s federal spending. Award activity in Maine counts 6,882 contracts, with an average award size of $400K. Obligations fell 29.9% year over year. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $2B. BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION is the largest vendor by obligations at $2B, followed by MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC at $433M. Smaller but notable buyers include the Department of Labor at $28M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $25M. Other industries with meaningful obligations include Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $99.1M and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $433M. FY2026 to date totals are partial; prior full-year obligations include FY25 at $3.9B and FY23 and FY24 at $3.7B each. This snapshot highlights concentration in defense shipbuilding and a small set of large vendors driving most federal dollars in Maine.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who is driving federal spending in Maine?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is driving federal spending in Maine, obligating $2.6B. Ship Building and Repairing is the leading industry, with $2B in obligations. BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION is the top vendor, with $2B in obligations. Other buyers with smaller roles include the Department of Labor at $28M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $25M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has Maine’s federal contracting trended recently?",
        "body": "Total federal contract obligations in Maine are $2.8B for FY2026 to date, down 29.9% year over year. FY25 obligations were $3.9B, and FY23 and FY24 obligations were $3.7B each. Award activity to date shows 6,882 contracts and an average award size of $400K."
      }
